        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Catherine the Great</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1729 CE – 1796 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">2 May 1729 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> (now Szczecin), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, as Princess Sophie Auguste Friederike von Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>; daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-august-prince-of-anhalt-zerbst">Christian August, Prince of Anhalt-Zerbst</a>, and Joanna Elisabeth of Holstein-Gottorp</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">17 November 1796, the evening after a stroke that left her in a coma; an autopsy confirmed the stroke as the cause</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Grand duchess as wife of the heir Peter (from 1745); empress consort (1762); Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 9 July 1762 until her death in 1796</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the coup of 1762 against Peter III; wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1783; the partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; the provincial reform of 1775 and the charters of 1785; the secularisation of Church lands; the Smolny Institute and a state school system; the Hermitage; her correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a></span></div>
