    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A German princess with no claim to the Russian throne, Catherine took it in 1762 by a coup against her husband Peter III, who died eight days later in unexplained circumstances.</li><li>In a reign of over 34 years she extended the empire by some 520,000 square kilometres at the expense mainly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, and reorganised provincial government, education and Church property.</li><li>She brought the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> into public debate, but the exploitation of serf labour intensified under her and was one of the chief reasons behind Pugachev's Rebellion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>To promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/variolation">smallpox inoculation</a>, then a controversial method, Catherine had herself inoculated in 1768 by the English doctor Thomas Dimsdale; by 1800 approximately 2 million inoculations had been administered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

