    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1729</span><span class="rf-text">Sophie of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, the future Catherine, is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, where her father is governor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1745</span><span class="rf-text">Having been received into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> as Catherine the year before, she marries the heir to the Russian throne, the future Peter III</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text">With the support of guards regiments she overthrows Peter III six months after his accession and begins her reign as Catherine II</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1764</span><span class="rf-text">She secularises the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> and has her former lover Stanislaus Augustus Poniatowski placed on the Polish throne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1774</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> ends a victorious war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>; Pugachev's Rebellion spreads among serfs and peasants</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1783</span><span class="rf-text">Catherine annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> becomes a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-georgievsk">Treaty of Georgievsk</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1795</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> divide the remaining territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in the third partition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1796</span><span class="rf-text">Catherine suffers a stroke and dies the following evening; her son succeeds as Paul I</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia.jpg" alt="Peter III of Russia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia">Peter III of Russia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronation_portrait_of_Peter_III_of_Russia_-1761.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Conrad Pfandzelt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Catherine was born on 2 May 1729 as Princess Sophie Auguste Friederike von Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, in Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Her father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-august-prince-of-anhalt-zerbst">Christian August, Prince of Anhalt-Zerbst</a>, of the ruling German family of Anhalt, was a Prussian general and governor of the city; her mother was Joanna Elisabeth of Holstein-Gottorp. The family had little money, and her rise was supported by her mother's wealthy relatives. Among the more than 300 sovereign entities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, small princely families advanced themselves by marriage, and Sophie was groomed throughout her childhood to become the wife of a powerful ruler. She was taught chiefly by a French governess and tutors, in etiquette, French and Lutheran theology. In 1739, aged ten, she met her second cousin, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia">Peter III of Russia</a>, whom she later wrote that she found detestable.</p><p>Her selection as bride of the heir was bound up with a diplomatic intrigue meant to strengthen the friendship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. She arrived in Russia in 1744, aged 15, and set out to win the Empress Elizabeth and the Russian people, studying Russian late into the night. During a grave illness she asked for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> priest rather than a Lutheran pastor, which raised her standing at court. Against her father's objections she was received into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> on 28 June 1744, taking the name Catherine Alekseyevna. She married Peter on 21 August 1745 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. Elizabeth expelled her mother from the country amid allegations of spying for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of Prussia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas de Largillière, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The marriage was unsuccessful and, by the usual account, remained unconsummated for years. The couple lived at Oranienbaum, where from 1746 Elizabeth placed Catherine under a supervision that one historian, Dixon, compares to virtual house arrest. Catherine read widely in French, among others <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>. She took lovers, among them Sergei Saltykov, Stanislaus Augustus Poniatowski and from 1759 the officer Grigory Orlov. Her son Paul was born in 1754 and taken into Elizabeth's care; the first version of her memoirs strongly implies that his father was Saltykov, while the final version explains why he was Peter's son. She gained political allies, among them the chancellor Alexey Bestuzhev-Ryumin, whose arrest in 1758 exposed her to scrutiny.</p><p>Elizabeth died on 5 January 1762 and Peter became emperor. His admiration for Frederick II, against whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had been fighting, his ending of operations against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and his planned war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> alienated the groups Catherine had cultivated. On the night of 8 July 1762 she learned that a co-conspirator had been arrested and that the coup must take place at once. The next day she went to the Izmailovsky Regiment and asked the soldiers to protect her, and then to the Semenovsky Barracks, where the clergy were waiting. She had Peter arrested and made him sign an abdication. On 17 July he died at Ropsha, possibly at the hands of Alexei Orlov; how he died is unknown, and the official cause was haemorrhoidal colic and a stroke. She was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 22 September 1762.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774.jpg" alt="Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torelli2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stefano Torelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Catherine extended the empire by some 520,000 square kilometres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1768–1774, with victories at Chesma and Kagul in 1770, ended in the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> territories on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and the position of protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. She annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1783, and a second war ended with the Treaty of Jassy in 1792. Cities such as Kherson, Nikolayev, Yekaterinoslav and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a> were founded in the south. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> she placed Poniatowski on the throne in 1764 and shared in the partitions of 1772, 1793 and 1795. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1783, and she mediated in the War of the Bavarian Succession.</p><p>In 1766 she summoned a commission of 652 members of all classes and wrote for it an Instruction drawn, as she admitted, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and Beccaria; it dissolved after more than 200 sittings without getting beyond theory. The Statute on provincial administration of 1775 divided the country into provinces and districts, 50 and nearly 500 by the end of the reign. In 1785 she issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-to-the-gentry">Charter to the Nobility</a> and the Charter of the Towns. A decree of 1764 transferred the Church's estates, with more than 911,000 registered male peasants, to the treasury.</p><p>She founded the Smolny Institute in 1764, approved a Statute of National Schools in 1786, under which the recorded schools rose to 316 with 17,341 pupils by 1796, permitted private <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> houses in 1783, issued the first government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> and began the Hermitage as her own collection. She corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> for 15 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Catherine's title was disputed from the start; historians debate whether her status was that of a regent or a usurper, tolerable only in her son's minority. Ivan VI, confined since infancy, was killed during an attempt to free him, on standing instructions she had given, as Elizabeth had before her. The greatest revolt was that of Pugachev, who claimed to be Peter III and drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, nomads, peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and peasants in 1774. Its causes lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>: the demands of state and landowners intensified the exploitation of serf labour, and nobles gained the power to send serfs to hard labour in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Catherine forbade serfs to petition her directly, and gave away serfs in great numbers, 100,000 on one day in 1795. After the uprising she was pushed away from the idea of liberation.</p><p>When Alexander Radishchev published his Journey from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1790, warning of uprisings over the condition of the serfs, she had the book burned and the author exiled to Siberia. She deported over 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a> to Siberia in 1764, closed 569 of 954 monasteries, confined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> acquired with Polish lands to the Pale of Settlement and taxed them at double the rate of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> neighbours. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> provoked the Confederation of Bar and, in 1794, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a>, both defeated.</p><p>The British ambassador James Harris credited her with a “masculine force of mind” but charged her with vanity and love of flattery. Her grandson Nicholas I judged her foreign policy dishonest, and it is criticised as lacking a long-term strategy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin.jpg" alt="Grigory Potemkin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin">Grigory Potemkin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princepotemkin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Johann Baptist von Lampi the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In her last years Catherine's favourite was Platon Zubov, 40 years her junior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin">Grigory Potemkin</a>, her lover and adviser, who had governed the colonisation of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, had died in 1791. In 1796 she made war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, whose new king Agha Mohammad Khan had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1795; on Zubov's advice she gave the command not to the seasoned general Ivan Gudovich but to Zubov's youthful brother Valerian, whose troops stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> in May and by mid-June had overrun most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><p>Her reign ended in two failures. In September 1796 the Swedish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iv-adolf">Gustav IV Adolf</a> visited her, and she intended her granddaughter Alexandra to become queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. He would not accept that Alexandra would remain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, stayed away from the ball of 11 September at which the engagement was to be announced, and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. The frustration affected Catherine's health. She recovered enough to plan a ceremony naming her favourite grandson Alexander as heir in place of her son Paul, but died before it could be announced.</p><p>On 16 November 1796 she rose early, took her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and sat down to her papers. Some time after nine she was found on the floor, her face purplish and her breathing laboured. The court physician diagnosed a stroke; she fell into a coma, received the last rites and died the following evening at about 9:45. An autopsy confirmed the cause. She was laid out with a gold crown on her head, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> brocade dress. Paul succeeded her and ordered the army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to retreat to Russia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denis-diderot.jpg" alt="Denis Diderot" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denis_Diderot_by_Louis-Michel_van_Loo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Catherine the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> was recognised as a great power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and she is often counted among the enlightened despots. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> ceased to exist as an independent nation until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The decline of Ottoman power opened the European contest over the Eastern Question, which lasted until that empire's dissolution. Paul's reversal of the Persian campaign embittered the Zubovs and other officers, many of whom were among the conspirators who arranged his murder five years later.</p><p>Her institutions outlasted her. The provincial boards of public welfare, the foundling homes and the school statute of 1786 gave public education a legal and administrative foundation for the nineteenth century, though the schools excluded serfs and most rural inhabitants had little access to them. The assignation roubles she introduced circulated until 1849. The Hermitage, begun as her personal collection, now occupies the whole Winter Palace, and the Imperial Public Library she founded in 1795 opened in 1814. Writers of her reign such as Derzhavin and Fonvizin prepared the ground for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> and d'Alembert cemented her reputation in their writings; Voltaire called her the “Star of the North”. Her sexual independence gave rise to many legends. Historians regard her promotion of inoculation as one of her most significant contributions, and note that she was the first Russian ruler to bring the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> into public debate while her economy and army continued to depend on it.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Catherine II (1729–1796), a German princess married to the heir to the Russian throne, overthrew her husband Peter III in 1762 and ruled for over 34 years. She expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and reformed provincial government, schools and Church property, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> grew harsher and provoked Pugachev's Rebellion.</em></p>

